  "text": "**Review of *The Great Gatsby***  \n\n*The Great Gatsby*, written by **F. Scott Fitzgerald**, is a timeless exploration of American dreams, love, and the tragic consequences of obsession. Set in the roaring twenties, the novel follows the mysterious **Jay Gatsby**, a wealthy man who throws extravagant parties in **West Egg**, Long Island. His mansion, located across the bay from **East Egg**, is a symbol of his lavish lifestyle and his desire to rekindle a romance with **Daisy Buchanan**, a woman from a more prominent social class.\n\nThe story is narrated by **Nick Carraway**, a young man who moves to **New York** and becomes Gatsby's closest confidant. Through Nick\u2019s perspective, we witness the lavish parties, the affairs, and the moral decay of the era. The contrast between **West Egg** and **East Egg** represents the divide between new money and old money, a central theme of the novel.\n\nAt the heart of the story is the tragic love story between Gatsby and Daisy, which is complicated by the presence of **Tom Buchanan**, Daisy\u2019s husband. Tom\u2019s arrogance and privilege, as well as Daisy\u2019s indecision, lead to Gatsby\u2019s downfall. The novel culminates in a tragic event that highlights the futility of Gatsby\u2019s dream and the inevitable consequences of living in the past.\n\nFitzgerald\u2019s prose is rich and evocative, capturing the essence of the Jazz Age with its themes of desire, illusion, and the American Dream. The novel\u2019s ending, with Gatsby\u2019s death and the haunting realization that his dream was never truly attainable, lingers long after the last page is turned.\n\n**Proper nouns from \"The Great Gatsby\" used in this review:**\n1. *The Great Gatsby*\n2. F. Scott Fitzgerald\n3. Jay Gatsby\n4. West Egg\n5. East Egg\n6. New York\n7. Nick Carraway\n8. Tom Buchanan\n9. Daisy Buchanan\n\n**Total: 9 proper nouns**.",
